WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
- In my administration
I'm going to enforce

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.690
all laws concerning the protection

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.140
of classified information.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.880
(audience cheering)

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.150
No one will be above the law.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:16.530
- Okay, now we know the crimes

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.590
that the DOJ thinks former
President Trump committed.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.210
When the FBI served a search warrant

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.040
on Donald Trump's Florida club,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.930
the reaction was shock and outrage.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.960
What records could Trump have
that would justify a raid

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.490
on the ex-president's home?

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.560
Well, now we know that reports indicate

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.450
that the FBI was sent
to retrieve documents

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.168
in part related to nuclear weapons.

00:00:36.168 --> 00:00:37.380
(bomb exploding)

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.570
And although we don't know
what was in the dozens of boxes

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.130
removed from Mar-a-Lago,

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:43.470
we do know that Jay Bratt
was present for the search.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.780
Bratt is the FBI's chief
of counterintelligence

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.070
and export control.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.590
That probably means that
yes, Trump had information

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.710
related to nuclear weapons
or national defense.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.240
This is a really big deal

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.250
because presidents do not
have the right to classify

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.920
or declassify records relating
to nuclear information.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.390
We'll get into that in a second.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.070
And now we have the benefit
of the actual warrant

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.230
that was served on Trump's estate.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.850
So in this video we're
gonna take a deep dive

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.610
into the crimes that
former President Trump

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.100
may have violated by taking
a U-Haul full of documents

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.320
to his basement in Palm Beach.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:14.640
But here's what we know so far.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.250
On Friday morning, Trump
sent messages by Truth Social

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:18.247
insisting that the, quote,

00:01:18.247 --> 00:01:20.640
"Nuclear weapons issue is a hoax."

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.970
He also suggested that
Barack Hussein Obama

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.250
had lots of nuclear information

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.290
that he had used for his library.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:31.260
He also once again accused the
FBI of planting information,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.000
and almost at the same time

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.710
he claimed that he had a standing order

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:38.100
to declassify all information
taken out of the White House,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.170
which seems to run
contrary to his allegation

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:41.760
that the FBI planted the information

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.310
that he seemingly declassified.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:45.480
It has been alleged that Mar-a-Lago

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.490
does have security cameras,
so there is a possibility

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.590
that the search might have been recorded.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.260
Now meanwhile, Eric Trump
did what Trumps do best.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.540
He asked people to donate
money if they're mad.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.040
And now after a week of speculation,

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.170
we have the actual search
warrant that was served,

00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.490
which is fairly unusual

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.800
because these things tend to stay sealed.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.470
So why was it the case
that the court unsealed it?

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:05.730
Well, according to "The New York Times,"

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.350
the government first
tried to convince Trump

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.690
to simply return the records at issue.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:10.650
When he did not comply,

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:12.210
the government served
Trump with a subpoena

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:13.290
to return the boxes.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:15.900
Trump apparently only partially
responded to the subpoena,

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.640
so the DOJ decided to get a search warrant

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.237
to get the rest of the records.

00:02:19.237 --> 00:02:20.730
"The Washington Post" has reported

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:22.590
the FBI sought, among other items,

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.050
classified documents
related to nuclear weapons.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.000
Attorney General Merrick
Garland held a press conference

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.130
on Thursday where he
confirmed three things.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.600
First, that he approved the warrant

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.820
in accordance with a memo
on political targets.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.200
For more information about that memo,

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.270
which originated with Trump's
attorney general, Bill Barr,

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.880
check out my other video on
the Trump search warrant.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.610
Now second, the DOJ filed a
motion with the district court

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:44.820
to unseal the warrant application
and the warrant itself.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.230
That made the two documents public,

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.990
although it did not unseal
the probable cause affidavit

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:52.230
that gave rise to the warrant itself.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.750
And third, the search
warrant and the return

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.400
were left with Trump's lawyers

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.560
in accordance with the federal
rules of civil procedure.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.930
So now we can examine the
unsealed warrant itself.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.130
According to the search warrant,

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:04.207
the property to be seized included, quote,

00:03:04.207 --> 00:03:06.607
"All physical documents and
records constituting evidence,

00:03:06.607 --> 00:03:08.167
"contraband, fruits of crime,

00:03:08.167 --> 00:03:09.697
"or other items illegally possessed

00:03:09.697 --> 00:03:14.697
"in violation of 18 U.S.C.
sections 793, 2071, or 1519."

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:17.790
So this means that Trump
is being investigated

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:19.080
under the following statutes:

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.930
18 U.S.C. 2071 which
prohibits the concealment,

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:24.092
removal, or mutilation of information,

00:03:24.092 --> 00:03:27.300
18 U.S.C. 793 which prohibits
gathering, transmitting,

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.980
or losing defense information,

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:32.520
and 18 U.S.C. 1519 which
outlaws the destruction,

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.440
alteration, or falsification of records

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.060
in federal investigations.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:37.320
Trump's lawyers have argued

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.960
that Trump had a right to
declassify all of these records

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.120
and that he did so before he left office.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.040
However, none of the
three statutes require

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:45.540
that the information be classified

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.190
in order for it to be a crime,

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.260
and we'll talk about
that wrinkle in a second.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.000
Trump's lawyer, Christina
Bobb, signed the receipt

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.550
listing the property that
was removed from Mar-a-Lago.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.650
The FBI took about 20 boxes of records,

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.230
photographs, binders,
and handwritten notes.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.590
The list starts with clemency
records related to Roger Stone

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.590
and, quote, "Info re:
the president of France."

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:07.290
They removed 11 sets of
classified documents.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.240
Some of these were marked top secret

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.070
and were only allowed to be stored

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:12.840
in special government facilities.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.400
It also says agents collected four sets

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:17.220
of top secret documents, three
sets of secret documents,

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.440
and three sets of confidential documents,

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.380
allowing the FBI to win their
game of federal gin rummy.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.390
Now the list of documents includes records

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:27.060
marked "Various classified
TS/SCI documents,"

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.220
and this abbreviation refers to top secret

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.470
and sensitive compartmented information.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.810
Now, sensitive compartmented
information is information

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.700
about certain intelligence
sources and methods

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.200
and can include information pertaining

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.350
to sensitive collection
systems, analytical processing,

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:43.080
and targeting, or things
that are derived from it.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.720
Now a person can only have access to SCI

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.970
if they have a top
secret security clearance

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:50.940
and if they have a
need-to-know designation.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:52.830
Although the president has access to SCI,

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:54.840
that doesn't necessarily
mean he has the authority

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.360
to remove SCI documents
and store them in his house

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.010
after he's no longer president.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:01.890
And since the boxes of records
recovered from Mar-a-Lago

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:04.020
do seem to relate to national defense,

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:06.750
I turn to one of the preeminent
national security lawyers

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.610
in the country and, coincidentally,

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:10.890
the lawyer who is currently
litigating my federal cases

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:12.900
regarding John Bolton's memoir

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:15.240
and the inspector general misconduct

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.210
in the last administration,
Kel McClanahan.

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:21.150
So Kel, what could it mean if Trump has

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.790
or withheld national security information?

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:26.100
- Well, once national security
information is involved,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.050
that's a completely different ball game.

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:30.390
And it doesn't even have to
be classified information.

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:31.470
The Espionage Act,

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:34.530
which I'm sure most of your
viewers are familiar with,

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:36.870
doesn't require something to be classified

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:40.710
before it heavily penalizes
people who take it, who hide it,

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:43.410
who destroy it, who keep
it in their basement.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:45.300
It just requires that something be called

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.600
national defense information,
which is helpfully defined

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:52.110
as information related
to the national defense.

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:54.870
That extraordinarily broad definition

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:58.290
allows people to be prosecuted
under the Espionage Act

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:01.200
for taking information
that is not classified

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.820
as long as it has something to
do with the national defense.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.100
And when you have classified information,

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:12.930
well, that triggers a different
law called 18 U.S.C. 1924

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:16.620
which used to be a misdemeanor
that people pled down to

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:19.230
for mishandling classified information,

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:23.220
but President Trump himself
signed a law in 2018

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:24.690
that made it a felony,

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:26.790
so that all they have to prove

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:29.940
is that there was classified information,

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:34.230
he did not store it properly,
therefore he's guilty.

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:36.330
It's as close to a strict liability crime

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:39.210
as you generally get in
the intelligence community.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:40.770
- And so Kel, there are also reports

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:43.410
that the documents could
relate to nuclear weapons.

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.530
If that's true, how
does that change things?

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:49.110
- Well, the involvement of
nuclear weapons information

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:51.660
throws a whole new variable into the mix.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:55.440
You see, what we think of
as classified information

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.680
is actually a creation
of an executive order.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:02.340
That is the control of the classification

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:04.470
is the exclusive domain of the president

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:05.940
and the executive branch.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:07.470
The president can classify it.

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.390
The president can declassify it.

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:11.730
However, nuclear weapons information

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:13.770
is one of the few types of information

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.660
that's controlled by a statute,

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:18.330
in this case the Atomic Energy Act,

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:21.390
and the statute says that this information

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:24.390
cannot be released or disseminated

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:27.390
without going through
a certain set of steps,

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:30.030
and that if not within
the exclusive control

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:31.140
of the president.

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:34.950
Therefore, even if Trump had said

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:37.380
that he declassified this information

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:40.650
before he took it home with
him, that wouldn't be enough.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:42.720
He would have declassified it

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.820
for the purposes of the executive order,

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:48.300
but he would still be bound
by the Atomic Energy Act

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:51.000
which carries its own criminal penalties.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:52.140
- Thanks a lot, Kel.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:54.180
Kel runs the National Security Counselor

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:55.170
and does great work,

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.870
including legal attempts to
recover the Secret Service

00:07:57.870 --> 00:07:59.520
and DHS leadership text messages

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.530
that were illegally deleted last year

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:03.360
that you've no doubt
read about in the news,

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:06.960
and a tax deductible donation
to the NSC would go a long way

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.400
since they're handling many cases,

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:09.750
including my own, pro bono.

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:11.910
So there's a link to that
down in the description.

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:13.680
So that's the information and documents

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:15.750
that are at issue in this investigation,

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:17.610
but let's look at the actual crimes

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:18.750
that are being investigated.

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:20.820
The first is 18 U.S.C. 1519,

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:23.070
the destruction, alteration,
or falsification of records

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:24.570
in federal investigations.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:27.097
It states, quote, "Whoever
knowingly alters, destroys,

00:08:27.097 --> 00:08:29.107
"mutilates, conceals,
covers up, falsifies,

00:08:29.107 --> 00:08:31.327
"or makes a false entry
in any record, document,

00:08:31.327 --> 00:08:34.537
"or tangible object with the
intent to impede, obstruct,

00:08:34.537 --> 00:08:35.797
"or influence the investigation

00:08:35.797 --> 00:08:37.327
"or proper administration of any matter

00:08:37.327 --> 00:08:39.577
"within the jurisdiction
of any department or agency

00:08:39.577 --> 00:08:41.977
"of the United States shall
be fined under this title,

00:08:41.977 --> 00:08:44.460
"imprisoned not more
than 20 years, or both."

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:46.620
So in other words, to convict
a defendant of obstruction

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.590
under section 1519, the
government has to prove

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:50.580
each of the following elements.

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:52.530
First, that the defendant
knowingly altered,

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:54.330
destroyed, concealed, or falsified

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:56.220
a record, document, or tangible object.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:59.400
And second, the defendant acted
with the intent to impede,

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:01.050
obstruct, or influence an actual

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:03.180
or contemplated investigation of a matter

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:06.240
within the jurisdiction of an
agency of the United States.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:07.620
Now, the government doesn't have to prove

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.830
the defendant's sole or
even primary intention

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:12.180
was to obstruct justice

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:14.610
as long as the government
proves beyond a reasonable doubt

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:16.530
that one of the defendant's intentions

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.060
was to obstruct justice.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:21.240
But that takes us to 8 U.S.C. 2071

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:23.340
which prohibits the willful
concealment, destruction,

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:25.290
or removal of federal documents.

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:27.180
To establish a violation
of this provision,

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:28.890
The government must prove, one,

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:31.890
an individual concealed,
removed, or destroyed a record

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:35.490
or attempted to do so or took
and carried away a record

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:36.990
with the intent to do so,

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:39.960
the record was filed or
deposited in a public office

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:40.793
of the United States,

00:09:40.793 --> 00:09:43.650
and the individual acted
willfully and unlawfully.

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:46.470
Now, note that this is
a specific intent crime

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:48.960
that requires a person
to have had an intention

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:52.500
to destroy or remove the records
in violation of these laws.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:55.230
And is often the case with
these specific intent crimes,

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:57.090
Trump might be able to
argue that he didn't know

00:09:57.090 --> 00:09:59.700
he wasn't allowed to remove
the documents or destroy them,

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:00.720
although there's certainly evidence

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:02.910
that he was repeatedly told
he was violating the law

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:04.717
with the way that he handled records.

00:10:04.717 --> 00:10:05.703
"The Washington Post" reported

00:10:05.703 --> 00:10:08.040
that two of Trump's
former chiefs of staff,

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:09.900
Reince Priebus and John Kelly,

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:12.000
and former White House Counsel Don McGahn,

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.640
warned him about the
Presidential Records Act.

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:18.660
When it comes to a potential
violation of 18 U.S.C. 2071,

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:20.130
there is an additional wrinkle

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:22.980
because that section
ostensibly prohibits someone

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:24.690
from running from office.

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:27.210
Now I take a deep dive into
that language, the case law,

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:30.270
and the potential repercussions
for former President Trump

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:31.920
in a video that's exclusive to Nebula

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:33.600
that I'll talk about in a little while.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:35.760
But that takes us to 18 U.S.C. 793

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:37.290
which concerns the
gathering, transmitting,

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:39.480
or losing defense information.

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:42.660
Section 793 is known as the Espionage Act

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:44.070
which covers the unlawful retention

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:45.660
of defense-related information

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:47.460
which could harm the
United States or an ally.

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:49.710
Now, the Espionage Act
is a bit of a misnomer.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:52.710
It's not really, or most of
it is not about espionage.

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:54.480
It's really about state secrets.

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:56.947
Now, section 793 D makes it
a crime for anyone, quote,

00:10:56.947 --> 00:10:59.647
"Lawfully having possession
of, access to, control over,

00:10:59.647 --> 00:11:02.227
"or being entrusted with any
of a range of tangible items

00:11:02.227 --> 00:11:04.057
"or information relating
to the national defense

00:11:04.057 --> 00:11:06.697
"which information the
possessor has reason to believe

00:11:06.697 --> 00:11:08.617
"could be used to the
injury of the United States

00:11:08.617 --> 00:11:10.927
"or to the advantage
of any foreign nation,

00:11:10.927 --> 00:11:12.877
"willfully communicates,
delivers, transmits,

00:11:12.877 --> 00:11:14.647
"or causes or attempts the same

00:11:14.647 --> 00:11:17.017
"to any person not entitled to receive it,

00:11:17.017 --> 00:11:19.897
"or willfully retains the
same and fails to deliver it

00:11:19.897 --> 00:11:22.267
"on demand to the officer or
employee of the United States

00:11:22.267 --> 00:11:23.780
"entitled to receive it."

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:24.960
In other words, it's a crime

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:27.270
to leak national security information.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:30.360
Section 793 F makes it a
crime for government officials

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.030
who have lawful possession of
such materials to remove them

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:35.040
or disclose them to third parties

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:36.720
who are unauthorized to receive them.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:37.770
To be guilty of this offense,

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:39.540
the person has to have
disclosed the information

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:41.190
due to their own gross negligence

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:42.960
or because of their failure to report

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:45.000
the information lost or stolen.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:47.340
But the Espionage Act is not
limited to just those people

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:50.340
who wrongfully disclose
national defense information.

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:53.070
Section 793 E also says
it applies to anyone

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:54.690
who knowingly disseminates, distributes,

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:57.210
or even retains national
defense information

00:11:57.210 --> 00:11:59.130
without immediately returning the material

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:01.790
to the government officer
authorized to possess it.

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:04.590
In other words, the text of
the act draws no distinction

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:06.600
between the leaker, the
recipient of the leak,

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:09.480
or the 100th person to
redistribute, retransmit,

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:11.490
or even retain the national
defense information

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:15.000
that, by that point, is
already in the public domain.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:17.160
So basically the entire
staff of Mar-a-Lago

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:19.110
should probably lawyer up at this point.

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.780
And if the person who is
charged under the act knows

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:23.880
or has reason to believe
that the information

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:26.430
in their possession relates
to the national defense

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:28.380
and could be used to
injure the United States

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:29.700
or benefit a foreign power,

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:31.980
that person's violating
the act's plain language

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:34.110
regardless of their specific intent.

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:35.730
So that means that almost
anyone in the chain

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:37.560
could be prosecuted under this section,

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:40.383
the leaker, who in this case
would be the ex-president,

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:44.370
his son, son-in-law, a journalist,

00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:46.650
or even one of Trump's lawyers.

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:48.660
Now there is one crime
that isn't mentioned

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:49.560
in the search warrant,

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:52.530
and that is a violation of
the Presidential Records Act,

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:53.850
and there's a very good reason

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:56.610
for why the PRA is not
mentioned in the search warrant.

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:57.930
So let's talk about a president

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:00.720
and former president's
responsibility under the PRA.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:03.450
Section 2203 of the
act gives the president

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:05.850
the responsibility of
identifying preserving records

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:06.810
during the administration

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:09.150
and requires the president
to take all such steps

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:11.820
as may be necessary to
preserve presidential records.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:14.520
However, a president
may also destroy records

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:15.847
that the president decides, quote,

00:13:15.847 --> 00:13:17.857
"No longer have
administrative, historical,

00:13:17.857 --> 00:13:20.250
"informational, or evidentiary value."

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:21.420
Before destroying records,

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:24.360
the president must ask the
archivist for their opinion.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.040
Although the archivist
can give an opinion,

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:27.450
the person can't stop the president

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:29.100
from making their own call.

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:30.990
And if the archivist does not sign off

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:32.460
on the president's determination,

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:34.290
congressional subcommittees are notified,

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:36.810
but it's unclear what
Congress can actually do

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:38.340
during such a dispute.

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:39.900
The statute is murky and vague,

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:42.270
which brings up the most
important point about the PRA.

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:44.550
It lacks a real enforcement mechanism.

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:46.710
Although a president is not
supposed to rip up records,

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:48.750
shred them, or flush them down the toilet,

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.630
the PRA is at most a
statement of best practices

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:52.800
like an honor system.

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:54.810
This was illustrated
early in Trump's tenure

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:58.500
when CREW challenged Trump's
record keeping practices

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:00.390
by suing him under the PRA.

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:02.190
CREW alleged that Trump deleted tweets

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:03.390
in violation of the PRA

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:05.580
and that the White
House's use of encrypted

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:08.610
and auto-deleting messages
hindered the ability

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:10.800
of federal agencies to
perform their duties.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:12.210
The lawsuit sought a court order

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:14.640
compelling the president to
stop auto deleting messages

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:16.320
and preserve them under the PRA,

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.090
but the DC circuit
dismissed CREW's lawsuit

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:20.670
interpreting the president's
record-keeping duties narrowly.

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:23.010
The court held that mandamus
relief was only available

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:24.540
under extraordinary circumstances

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:27.090
and that a president's
record-keeping duties under the PRA

00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:30.210
are to discretionary to be
enforced by court order.

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:32.040
Although courts can
review the classification

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:33.150
of existing records,

00:14:33.150 --> 00:14:35.070
a court can't stop the
president from acting.

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:36.600
In other words, the court held

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.010
it could not tell the president

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:40.650
to create and preserve particular records.

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:42.870
However, courts have
been willing to enforce

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:45.660
some modest limits on what
White House officials can do

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:48.210
with records that have
already been created.

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:50.730
CREW successfully sued Dick
Cheney for failure to classify

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:52.950
some documents as presidential records.

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:55.110
A district court also
invalidated an agreement

00:14:55.110 --> 00:14:57.840
between President H.W. Bush
and the National Archives

00:14:57.840 --> 00:14:59.430
that would've conferred complete control

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:01.140
of presidential records on the president

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:02.730
after he left office.

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:05.280
Although citizens don't have
a right to enforce the PRA,

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:07.560
the Department of Justice
arguably does have a mandate

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:10.200
to enforce the PRA in some circumstances.

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:11.580
CREW and the National Security Archive

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:14.940
wrote to FBI director Chris
Wray and AG Merrick Garland

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:16.740
and requested that they investigate

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:18.870
whether former President
Trump violated criminal laws

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:20.310
by willfully destroying and mutilating

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:22.350
his presidential records while in office.

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:23.940
The National Archives also requested

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:26.580
that the Department of Justice
undertake an investigation.

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:28.200
But there are a number
of criminal statutes

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:29.550
that make removal and destruction

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:31.200
of federal property a crime.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:33.180
It is generally illegal to remove property

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:34.560
from the White House.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:37.170
For example, in 2001, Bill
Clinton staffers removed

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:39.570
the W key from West Wing keyboards

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:42.360
right before George W.
Bush was inaugurated.

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:44.250
This prank was actually theft,

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:45.930
and the general accounting
office interviewed

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:49.200
over 100 government employees
about the theft of the Ws.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:51.630
So if the government
decides to charge Trump

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:53.730
with crimes related to these documents,

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:55.740
what are Trump's defenses here?

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:58.590
Well, the first would
probably be declassification.

00:15:58.590 --> 00:16:00.360
Trump's lawyers say
that he had declassified

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:01.680
the documents at issue.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:03.930
Now, presidents do have
broad plenary power

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:06.150
to classify and declassify documents.

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:09.360
That power stems from their
position as commander-in-chief.

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:11.550
The president can delegate this authority,

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:13.530
they can exercise it themselves,

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:15.900
or they can order the release
of classified documents

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:17.640
after a certain period of time.

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:19.830
And as far as we know, Barack
Obama was the last president

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:22.530
to issue an order on
classified information.

00:16:22.530 --> 00:16:25.800
Under Obama's Executive Order 13526,

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:27.390
original classification authorities,

00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:29.550
like the heads of various
departments and agencies,

00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:31.740
were allowed to classify
and declassify information

00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:33.720
owned by their organizations.

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:35.670
That means that
departments might authorize

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:37.740
the disclosure of information to someone

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:39.480
with the proper security clearance.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:41.640
And this executive order
has not been rescinded

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:43.830
and federal agencies
are still following it.

00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:45.510
However, future and following presidents,

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:46.560
like Trump and Biden,

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:49.080
are under no obligation to
follow Obama's executive order.

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:51.120
They can tweak the order, issue their own,

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:53.490
or simply handle things
in an ad hoc manner.

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:55.770
And Trump's approach to
classified information

00:16:55.770 --> 00:16:58.410
could probably be
described as freewheeling.

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:00.510
And after Trump lost the 2020 election,

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:02.677
Donald Trump Jr. tweeted
that his dad should, quote,

00:17:02.677 --> 00:17:03.877
"Declassify everything.

00:17:03.877 --> 00:17:06.420
"We can't let the bad
actors get away with it."

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:08.400
Could Trump have declassified everything?

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:09.810
- I think deepen your heart

00:17:09.810 --> 00:17:11.790
you already know the answer to that.

00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:13.710
- Well, technically, yes, the
president does have the power

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:16.440
to declassify every document
that the U.S. government holds,

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:18.870
but then that information would be public

00:17:18.870 --> 00:17:21.570
and you could FOIA that
information and release it.

00:17:21.570 --> 00:17:23.700
And Trump's defenders in
Congress held a press conference

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:25.830
to explain that not
everything that is classified

00:17:25.830 --> 00:17:27.540
is totally classified,

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:29.700
and in some ways what they're saying

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:30.720
actually does make sense.

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:32.880
The government tends to
overclassify records,

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:34.770
and arguably a lot more information

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:36.978
should be available to the public.

00:17:36.978 --> 00:17:40.200
Transparency in government
is generally a good thing,

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:41.880
but in this case we may be talking about

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:44.600
some extremely sensitive
top secret information,

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:47.760
information that may
relate to nuclear weapons.

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:49.590
And this gets us to one
of the main exceptions

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:51.390
about the president's
declassification powers

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:53.760
which is that the president
doesn't fully control

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:55.530
information about nuclear weapons.

00:17:55.530 --> 00:17:57.810
The Atomic Energy Act
regulates information

00:17:57.810 --> 00:17:59.850
about nuclear weapons and nuclear energy.

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:02.610
Under the act, restricted data
and formally restricted data

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:04.320
are top secret information,

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:06.090
and the act defines restricted data

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:08.220
as all data concerning
the design, manufacturer,

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:10.140
or utilization of atomic weapons,

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:12.120
the production of
special nuclear material,

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:14.220
and the use of special nuclear material

00:18:14.220 --> 00:18:15.780
in the production of energy.

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:17.220
Now, nuclear information is placed

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:19.080
in the formally restricted data category

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:21.300
after the Department of Energy
and the Department of Defense

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:23.610
jointly determine that
it relates primarily

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:25.770
to the military utilization
of atomic weapons.

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:27.960
Now, the president also lacks
control over information

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:30.720
related to U.S. and foreign
intelligence operations.

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:33.420
The Intelligence Identities
Protection Act protects

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:36.420
the identity of covert U.S.
intelligence operatives.

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:38.370
Their identities and
other intelligence sources

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:39.420
are protected by statute

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:40.620
and the president cannot reveal them

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:41.940
without breaking federal law.

00:18:41.940 --> 00:18:43.470
And there's also another exception

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:45.600
to the president's declassification power

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:47.220
which should be completely obvious.

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:49.260
Former presidents don't
have the legal authority

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:51.660
to declassify records
after they leave office.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:53.430
But at base, as noted earlier,

00:18:53.430 --> 00:18:56.430
declassifying the documents
is only one of the hurdles

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:59.070
since a lot of the statutes at issue here

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:01.890
do not turn on whether the
information is classified.

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:03.690
That's completely unrelated.

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:05.760
We also don't know if Trump
followed the procedure

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:07.290
for declassifying the documents.

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:09.660
He can't just think
declassification in his head

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:12.660
while president and the
documents are declassified.

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:15.330
The lawyers at Just Security
summarize the process

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:17.287
for declassification like this:

00:19:17.287 --> 00:19:19.477
"Classified documents have
classification markings

00:19:19.477 --> 00:19:21.367
"in the header and footer of each page

00:19:21.367 --> 00:19:23.047
"indicating the level of classification

00:19:23.047 --> 00:19:24.517
"for the document as a whole.

00:19:24.517 --> 00:19:26.977
"Furthermore, classified
records have cover sheets

00:19:26.977 --> 00:19:29.647
"that specifically indicate
when the record was classified,

00:19:29.647 --> 00:19:31.387
"by whom, and under what authority,

00:19:31.387 --> 00:19:34.057
"as well as when the
classification expires.

00:19:34.057 --> 00:19:36.187
"If Trump did in fact
order the declassification,

00:19:36.187 --> 00:19:37.717
"he still needed to make sure his staff

00:19:37.717 --> 00:19:39.217
"took the necessary next steps

00:19:39.217 --> 00:19:41.917
"to modify the classification
markings on the documents

00:19:41.917 --> 00:19:43.927
"before he could actually
handle and store the records

00:19:43.927 --> 00:19:47.197
"as a private citizen as
if they were unclassified.

00:19:47.197 --> 00:19:49.147
"Under security classification rules,

00:19:49.147 --> 00:19:51.577
"a classification marking on
a document has to be treated

00:19:51.577 --> 00:19:53.677
"as valid and binding unless and until

00:19:53.677 --> 00:19:55.477
"a subsequent marking replaces it.

00:19:55.477 --> 00:19:57.157
"Appropriate government
staffers would've needed

00:19:57.157 --> 00:19:58.837
"to cross out the classification markings

00:19:58.837 --> 00:20:01.747
"in the headers and footers
and stamped 'declassified'

00:20:01.747 --> 00:20:04.957
"on the record noting when
it was declassified, by whom,

00:20:04.957 --> 00:20:06.427
"and under what authority.

00:20:06.427 --> 00:20:08.287
"Since that does not
appear to have been done

00:20:08.287 --> 00:20:10.447
"with the classified documents
reportedly identified

00:20:10.447 --> 00:20:12.427
"to date, the documents remain classified

00:20:12.427 --> 00:20:14.047
"and had to be treated as classified

00:20:14.047 --> 00:20:16.230
"for handling and storage purposes."

00:20:16.230 --> 00:20:17.580
It's probably worth noting that ignorance

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:20.610
is not gonna be a defense here
because reportedly the FBI

00:20:20.610 --> 00:20:23.970
and DOJ met with Trump several
times, issued a subpoena,

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:26.520
and not all of the
documents were given back.

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:28.560
And even the fact that
some of the documents

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:30.240
were given back initially

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:33.990
shows that they know how to
handle classified documents

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:36.240
and agree that some of these documents

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:38.100
needed to be returned to the government.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:40.740
That being said, Trump might
have a First Amendment defense.

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:42.750
The Supreme Court does
recognize that in some cases

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:44.370
the public's interest
in receiving information

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:46.920
can outweigh the government's
interest in keeping it secret.

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:49.350
However, when evaluating
First Amendment concerns

00:20:49.350 --> 00:20:51.120
the court gives more leeway to reporters

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:52.860
who lawfully obtain government documents

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:54.240
